---
import ContentLayout from '../../layouts/ContentLayout.astro';
import { routePath } from '../../i18n/routes';

const locale = 'en' as const;
const p = (key: Parameters<typeof routePath>[1]) => routePath(locale, key);

const features = [
  { label: 'TEST', title: 'Search your name first', text: 'Search your name in quotes. If no profile card appears on the right, your profile isn’t live, and it’s too early to look at rankings.' },
  { label: 'VERIFICATION', title: 'Unverified means invisible', text: 'If the dashboard says “Verification required” or “Under review”, the profile won’t appear in search, however complete it is.' },
  { label: 'CATEGORY', title: 'The first suspect: category', text: 'If you show up for your name and not in a service search, check your primary category. Compare it with your competitors in the top three.' },
  { label: 'DISTANCE', title: 'Where the searcher stands', text: 'Map rankings change with where the searcher is standing. Checking from inside your business on your own phone misleads you.' },
];

const toc = [
  { id: 'iki-durum', label: 'Two different problems' },
  { id: 'yayinda', label: 'Is the profile live?' },
  { id: 'askiya', label: 'Suspension' },
  { id: 'kopya', label: 'Duplicate profile' },
  { id: 'kategori', label: 'Wrong category' },
  { id: 'ilk-uc', label: 'Behind the top three' },
  { id: 'site', label: 'Regular results' },
];

const faq = [
  {
    q: 'I can see my profile, so why can’t my customers?',
    a: 'Because Google shows you your own business more readily, and results change with the searcher’s location. To see the real picture, check in an incognito tab, signed out, from a point far from your business. To see your whole area, you need measurement from different points around you.',
  },
  {
    q: 'I just created my profile. How long should I wait?',
    a: 'After verification, a profile takes anywhere from a few days to a few weeks to settle, and rankings fluctuate during that time. Don’t touch your name, address or category in the first weeks. Disturbing a profile while it settles delays the recovery.',
  },
  {
    q: 'If I hide my address, will I show up across a wider area?',
    a: 'No. A profile without an address falls behind in “near me” searches, and Google still calculates rankings from proximity to your hidden address. If you have a place where you receive customers, show the address.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Will I show up if I add a keyword to my business name?',
    a: 'You might for a short while, but it breaks Google’s rules: the name must be the one you use in the real world. When a competitor reports it, the name gets corrected, and a repeat can get the profile suspended. We don’t do this on our clients’ profiles.',
  },
];
---

<ContentLayout
  locale={locale}
  route="guides/not-showing"
  title="Business Not Showing on Google: 8 Causes and Fixes | Wiserrise"
  description="If your business isn’t showing on Google Maps or in search, the cause is one of eight. Here are the checks to run and the order to run them in."
  eyebrow="GUIDE · VISIBILITY"
  h1="Why isn’t your business showing up on Google?"
  crumb="Not showing on Google"
  lede="If your business isn’t showing up on Google, there are two possibilities: either your profile isn’t live, or it’s live and stuck behind the top three. You can tell which one in a minute by searching your name. Below, we rule out eight causes one by one, starting with the quickest to check."
  schemaType="article"
  datePublished="2026-08-01"
  dateModified="2026-11-06"
  breadcrumb={[
    { label: 'Home', href: '/' },
    { label: 'Guides', href: p('guides') },
  ]}
  features={features}
  toc={toc}
  faq={faq}
  related={[
    { label: 'Profile verification problems', href: p('guides/verification'), text: 'If the postcard didn’t arrive or the video was rejected, we cover what to do in each case.' },
    { label: 'Ranking higher on Google Maps', href: p('guides/rank-higher'), text: 'The order in which to close the gap with your competitors.' },
    { label: 'How do you create a Google Business Profile?', href: p('guides/create-profile'), text: 'If you have no profile at all, the setup steps from the address decision to day one.' },
    { label: 'Google Business Profile Management', href: p('services/gbp'), text: 'We manage your category, service structure, reviews and posts together.' },
  ]}
  relatedHeading="Related pages"
  ctaLabel="Audit your profile for free"
  ctaHref={p('tools/gbp-audit')}
>
  <h2 id="iki-durum">Missing entirely, or just ranking lower?</h2>

  <p>
    “I’m not showing up on Google” describes two separate problems, and their fixes look nothing alike. First, find out
    which one you have.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say you run a business called Ege Yıldız Oto Ekspertiz in Bornova. When you type “Ege Yıldız Oto Ekspertiz”, your
    profile appears on the right. When you type “vehicle inspection Bornova”, you’re missing from the three businesses on
    the map.
  </p>

  <p>
    That’s a ranking problem. Your profile is in place; it’s just falling behind your competitors.
  </p>

  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>You don’t show up even for your name.</strong> The profile either doesn’t exist, isn’t verified, has been
      suspended or has a duplicate. The first three sections below are for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>You show up for your name and are missing from service searches.</strong> Go to the sections on category,
      distance and the gap with your competitors.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Don’t test on your own phone.</strong> Google shows the account linked to a profile its own business more
      readily, and the result changes with where you are. Check in an incognito tab, signed out, from somewhere far from
      your business.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="yayinda">Is your profile actually live?</h2>

  <p>
    For the quickest check, search your business name on Google in quotes, exactly as it appears on your sign. If no
    profile card appears on the right, the profile was either never created or never went live.
  </p>

  <p>
    This happens more than you’d think: the owner believes the profile is set up, and the process actually stopped
    halfway. If you have no profile at all, go to the <a href={p('guides/create-profile')}>profile creation guide</a>.
  </p>

  <p>
    If the profile exists, open the dashboard. If you see a “Verification required” or “Under review” warning, the
    profile won’t appear in search, however carefully it was filled in. If the postcard didn’t arrive or the video was
    rejected, we cover each case separately in the <a href={p('guides/verification')}>verification problems guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="askiya">Could your profile be suspended?</h2>

  <p>
    It could, and most owners notice late, because they never see a notification. A suspended profile disappears from
    search entirely, and the dashboard says “Suspended”.
  </p>

  <p>The most common causes of suspension:</p>

  <ul class="checklist">
    <li>Adding keywords to the business name (like “Ege Yıldız Oto Ekspertiz Bornova Damage Assessment”)</li>
    <li>Using a virtual office or a mailbox address</li>
    <li>Changing the name, address and category several times in a short period</li>
    <li>Opening more than one profile at the same address</li>
    <li>Operating in a field, or with a service description, that goes against Google’s rules</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    The order of work is clear: fix the cause first, then submit a review request. A request submitted before the fix
    only drags the process out.
  </p>

  <p>
    Opening a new profile is the worst move. The new listing gets flagged as a duplicate and suspended too, and you end up
    with two problem profiles. We cover the appeal steps in the <a href={p('guides/verification')}>verification
    guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kopya">Does the same business have two profiles?</h2>

  <p>
    If there are two profiles for the same business, Google can’t decide which one to show and weakens both. Hardly anyone
    creates a duplicate on purpose: a former owner, an employee or a directory’s automatic listing made it.
  </p>

  <p>
    To check, search Google Maps for your name and then, separately, for your phone number. If a duplicate turns up,
    report it from the correct profile and ask for the two to be merged. Merging is safer than deleting, because the
    merged profile carries its reviews over.
  </p>

  <h2 id="kategori">Does your category match the service being searched?</h2>

  <p>
    If you show up for your name and are missing from service searches, the first place we look is the primary category.
    This is where Google gets most of its answer to “what does this business do?”. If the category is wrong, you never
    enter the race for that service’s searches.
  </p>

  <p>
    Say you’re a lawyer who also works as a mediator, and you chose “Mediator” as your primary category. That choice pushes
    you back in “lawyer” searches, which carry the real volume. Physiotherapists make the same mistake with “Massage
    therapist”, and medical aesthetics practices with “Beauty salon”.
  </p>

  <p>
    The check is simple: search your target query on Google Maps and look at the categories of the businesses in the top
    three. If yours is different, you’ve most likely found the cause. We explain how we set up category and service
    structure on the <a href={p('services/gbp')}>Google Business Profile management</a> page.
  </p>

  <h2 id="ilk-uc">Showing up, just outside the top three?</h2>

  <p>
    If you’re fourth on the map, in practice you’re invisible, because the map box shows three businesses. Here we look
    at two things: how far the searcher is from you and the gap between you and your competitors.
  </p>

  <h3>How far is the searcher from you?</h3>

  <p>
    In local rankings, distance is decisive. It’s normal to be missing when someone searches from the other side of the
    city: at that point, there are businesses closer to them.
  </p>

  <p>
    The real question is where you rank in the area your customers can actually come from. A vehicle inspection business
    can rank first outside its own door and miss the top twenty 4 km away, and you can’t see that by checking from a single
    point. We see where you rank from different points around you with
    <a href={p('services/rank-tracking')}>measurement across your area</a>.
  </p>

  <h3>Where are you behind your competitors?</h3>

  <p>
    Put your profile side by side with the businesses in the top three. Compare these: review count and average rating,
    profile age, how fresh the photos and posts are, how broad the service list is, and which searches the website shows
    up for.
  </p>

  <p>
    In the profiles we look at, the gap shows up most in review count. We explain the order for closing the gap in the
    <a href={p('guides/rank-higher')}>guide to ranking higher on Google Maps</a>, and the review side in the
    <a href={p('guides/more-reviews')}>reviews guide</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="site">Missing from the results below the map too?</h2>

  <p>
    The causes so far were about the map. If you’re also missing from the regular results below the map, look for the
    problem on your website: the pages may not be indexed, they may be blocked by mistake, or the site may not be
    crawlable.
  </p>

  <p>
    For the quickest check, type “site:yourdomain.com” into Google. If no results appear, your site isn’t in the index.
    We cover the causes and the order of fixes on the <a href={p('services/technical-seo')}>technical SEO</a> page.
  </p>

  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Don’t change everything on the same day.</strong> Changing the name, address and category several times in a
      short period is one of the causes of suspension. After each fix, wait a few days and measure the result, and only
      then move on to the next one.
    </p>
  </div>

  <Fragment slot="cta">
    <h2><span class="highlight">Where</span> is your profile falling short?</h2>
    <p>
      The free audit tool scores your profile in 10 questions across category, reviews, freshness and the website link.
      You see the result on screen right away, and we don’t ask for your
      <span style="white-space: nowrap;">email address</span>.
    </p>
  </Fragment>
</ContentLayout>
